        "titleEn": "Compare monoprotic and diprotic curves using supplied data only",
        "materialEn": "Explicitly synthetic ideal 25 °C model: 25.00 mL contains 2.500 mmol total glycine and 2.500 mmol Cl⁻. The starting form is denoted H₂G⁺ but equilibrium redistribution is allowed. pKa1=2.34 for H₂G⁺/HG±, pKa2=9.60 for HG±/G⁻. Titrate with 0.1000 mol/L NaOH; additive volumes, Kw=10⁻¹⁴, γ=1. HG± is H₃N⁺–CH₂–COO⁻, H₂G⁺ is H₃N⁺–CH₂–COOH and G⁻ is H₂N–CH₂–COO⁻. The attached table is generated from mass/charge balance, not measured. Compare a monoprotic HA with equal formal amount and pKa=4.76, requiring only one OH⁻ equivalent. The complete glycine table and fullT05-C2monoprotic table are supplied model data.This task requires no pH or differential-capacity calculation.",
        "taskDemandEn": [
          "Plot or read the supplied model curves,mark the two visible glycine stages compared with one forHA,and assignH2G+/HG±andHG±/G−.",
          "Qualitatively compare buffer regions,total neutralisation and identification limits.Explain whypH6is not the prominent glycine buffer region."
        ],
        "workedModelEn": [
          "Glycine shows stages near25and50mLwith different conjugate pairs;the supplied monoprotic curve has one near25mL.The first glycine equivalence contains the ampholyte,not a second independent sample.",
          "Flatter regions lie near the suppliedpKvalues,withpH6between them.Glycine consumes two formal equivalents rather than one.Curves support class/species comparison,not unique molecular identification or a claim that every polyprotic acid displays two separated stages."
        ]
      }
